yupiter documentation
======================

Every document in this directory is plain text, in the style of the
top-level README, and describes one aspect of the repository.  Begin
with "repository", which maps the whole tree, then read whichever part
you need.


Documents
---------

repository
    Every top-level file and directory, what it contains, and which
    document describes it in more detail.

building
    How to configure, build, install and consume the library, what the
    build options do, why the compiler flags differ between the
    library and the tests, and what a port to another operating system
    or architecture has to provide.

testing
    The test programs, how each kind of test is verified, how to
    regenerate a golden file, and the rules a new test has to follow.

formatting
    The formatting engine behind yupiter_printf: the dialect it
    implements, how one call flows through the code, and where it
    refuses to produce output.

io
    Streams and their staging buffer, the operating-system layer with
    its system-call numbers, and the assembly files that talk to the
    kernel.

heap
    The dynamic memory allocator: the arena, the block layout, the
    allocation and release algorithms, the safe variants and their
    failure modes, and the accounting the tests rely on.

python-binding
    Python binding of the public C17 interface, built with PyO3
    and maturin, linking against libyupiter.so.


Reading order for a first-time reader
-------------------------------------

    repository -> building -> testing

and then, depending on the task:

    formatting or heap -> io -> building


Conventions used in these documents
-----------------------------------

  * Commands are run from the repository root unless stated
    otherwise.
  * The public interface and the C test programs are ISO C17, and the
    library implementation behind that interface is ISO C++23; assembly
    is GNU assembler (GAS) syntax.
  * A document never overrides AGENTS.md or the public comments in
    "<yupiter.h>".  When they disagree, the header is the contract:
    fix the document.
